//An outline, not yet an essay. The arguments are yours to write.//

!! Start from two things side by side

* [[Walker Expanded]] (2005): a type system of words and patterns, built to keep changing. It came out of [[Andrew Blauvelt]]'s studio three years before he wrote [[Towards Relational Design]]. I held it up as a model in my own 2009 thesis essay, [[Some Vernacular Design Principles (& their application today)]].
* The [[Oka Chair]]: its shape came from the lumber it could get.

Both were made to respond to their context. (One of those contexts has soil in it.)

!! Three answers to "what makes design good?"

* ''Form'' ([[Modernism]]): good is rational and universal, the same everywhere. Blauvelt's "syntax."
* ''Content'' ([[Postmodernism]]): good means something and questions something. Blauvelt's "semantics." See [[Deconstruction and Graphic Design]].
* ''Context'' ([[Contextual Era]]): good is what works where it lands. Blauvelt's "pragmatics."

Which chair is each era's example? (The Week 4 chair notes are the cast.)

!! Where the third answer runs out

See [[Contextual Era]]: Blauvelt's context is people and networks, not places.

!! Utopia is no place

[[Stephen Duncombe]], in [[Utopia is No Place]], interviewed for the Walker's [[Open Field]] (2012). Thomas More's pun: //utopia// is "no place" (ou-topos) and also sounds like "good place" (eu-topos). Duncombe wants utopia to stay a no-place: "a stimulus, a prompt but not a plan."

A bioregion is the opposite move. It puts the good place in one real place.

* Does that make bioregional design a blueprint, the thing Duncombe warns against? Or is one place a prompt for other places?
* Duncombe's worry is "a million and one individual and possibly incommensurate visions." That is also a description of [[Scaling Out]]: many small local versions instead of one big one.
* [[Pragmatic Utopian]]: make the dream real out of what is already around.

!! Where it could end

Is a bioregional era a fourth era, or just the third one taken seriously?

!! Background

* Your own starting point: [[Form <> Content <> Context]] (2021), "there is no universal style." Also [[SDCDC002: Contextual Design]], the older draft. It is close to the chapter, so write fresh rather than adapting it.
* Nicolas Bourriaud, //Relational Aesthetics// (1998; English 2002): art as the making of relations between people. Blauvelt borrows the word "relational" but never cites him. Bringing him in is your move, not Blauvelt's. Are Bourriaud's relations, like Blauvelt's, only between people?

* Read [[Towards Relational Design]] in grad school at [[MICA]], where [[Ellen Lupton]] was teaching the same ideas.
* Three Walker connections: Blauvelt's essay, the identity his studio made, and Duncombe's interview in //Open Field//.
